{
  "$description": "FAU Cybersecurity Club — \"hacker\" variant\n\nFour colors, four jobs:\n  green  #00BB00  identity  — code, labels, h3\n  blue   #1E90FF  structure — nav, edges, links, h2\n  pale   #D6E8DC  reading   — long-form body copy\n  red    #CC0000  here/now  — active nav, next meeting, hover, warnings\n\nRed is budgeted: max 3 instances per viewport. See design-spec §0.1.\nThis file is TOKENS ONLY. Structural CSS lives in assets/css/custom.css.\n(Hugo wraps this file in :root[data-r-theme-variant='hacker'] using native\n CSS nesting, so any non-:root selector here becomes a nested descendant.)",
  "$extensions": {
    "csc": {
      "variants": {
        "hacker": {},
        "hacker-light": {
          "$description": "FAU Cybersecurity Club — \"hacker-light\" variant\n\nThe daylight twin of theme-hacker.css. Same four jobs, same identity:\n  green  identity  — code, labels, h3\n  blue   structure — nav, edges, links, h2\n  ink    reading   — long-form body copy\n  red    here/now  — active nav, next meeting, hover, warnings\n\nWhat changes and why:\n  1. Every hue is DARKENED. #1E90FF is 3.0:1 on white and fails AA; #00BB00\n     is 2.4:1 and fails badly. Both are fine on black and unusable on paper.\n     Every colour below was measured against the ground: the lowest is\n     5.07:1, so all of them clear AA for normal text.\n  2. FAU red stays #CC0000 — 5.5:1 here, so the true brand red is legible as\n     text and the lifted red the dark variant needs is not required.\n  3. GLOW BECOMES SHADOW. A halo is light spilling off a lit surface; on\n     paper the same shape reads as a smudge. The bloom tokens resolve to\n     soft neutral shadows, and the headline halo resolves to transparent.\n  4. The hero does NOT flip — see THE STAGE below.\n\nThis file is TOKENS ONLY. Only one variant stylesheet is active at a time,\nso every token must exist here. A literal colour is set for this variant or\nmarked `inherit` in tokens/tokens.json (the generator refuses it\notherwise); an alias or non-colour token falls back to hacker's. Structural\nCSS lives in assets/css/custom.css.\n(Hugo wraps this file in :root[data-r-theme-variant='hacker-light'] using\n native CSS nesting, so any non-:root selector here becomes a descendant.)"
        },
        "cyber": {
          "$description": "FAU Cybersecurity Club — \"cyber\" variant\n\nThe third variant. Same four jobs as theme-hacker.css, but the green and\nthe blue swap rank:\n  green  identity AND structure — edges, ticks, glow, grid, code, h3\n  cyan   secondary — links, nav, h2, buttons, tags\n  pale   reading   — long-form body copy\n  red    here/now  — active nav, next meeting, hover, warnings\n\nWhat changes and why:\n  1. GREEN CARRIES THE LINEWORK. In the `hacker` variant every panel border,\n     corner tick, halo, wash and floor grid is blue and green is reserved for\n     code and h3. Here every one of those derived alphas is green, so the\n     whole page frame reads as phosphor. That is the entire point of this\n     variant — it is not `hacker` with a different accent.\n  2. THE SECONDARY IS CYAN, NOT MORE GREEN. Links, h2 and nav hover need to\n     be findable against a page whose furniture is already green. Cyan is far\n     enough away in hue to separate at a glance and close enough to keep the\n     screen coherent. Do not \"simplify\" it back to one green.\n  3. BRIGHTER PHOSPHOR. #00FF41 against a near-black ground is 14.2:1, well\n     above the 5.6:1 that #00BB00 gives in `hacker`. The ground drops with it\n     (#010805 rather than #030A08) so the lift reads as glow, not glare.\n  4. RED IS UNCHANGED AND STILL BUDGETED. FAU red stays the here/now colour,\n     max 3 instances per viewport. See design-spec §0.1.\n\nThis file is TOKENS ONLY. Only one variant stylesheet is active at a time,\nso every token must exist here. A literal colour is set for this variant or\nmarked `inherit` in tokens/tokens.json (the generator refuses it\notherwise); an alias or non-colour token falls back to hacker's. Structural\nCSS lives in assets/css/custom.css.\n(Hugo wraps this file in :root[data-r-theme-variant='cyber'] using native\n CSS nesting, so any non-:root selector here becomes a nested descendant.)"
        }
      }
    }
  },
