    "stage": {
      "$description": "THE STAGE — The home hero has its own small palette, separate from the page, because it is a composed picture rather than a surface: a ground, a floor grid, two fog lights, a scrim and the owl on top. Keeping those in their own token set means the hero can be tuned without touching anything else. The light variant redefines every one of them.",
      "$extensions": {
        "csc": {
          "variants": {
            "hacker-light": {
              "$description": "THE STAGE — The hero in daylight. Same composition as the dark variant — ground, floor grid, two fog lights, a scrim, the owl — rebuilt for paper:\n\n* the ground goes white, so the hero reads as a raised panel lifted off the page rather than a hole cut into it;\n* the owl's highlight shapes invert to ink, exactly as they do in images/owl-mark-light.svg — on a dark screen they are light catching an edge, on paper the same job is done by ink;\n* the scrim that fades the grid out goes white instead of black;\n* the glows become soft shadows, and the headline halo goes away entirely. Paper does not glow.\n* the owl sits at higher opacity: dark linework on white needs less help than bright linework on black."
            },
            "cyber": {
              "$description": "THE STAGE — The home hero has its own small palette, separate from the page, because it is a composed picture rather than a surface: a ground, a floor grid, two fog lights, a scrim and the owl on top. Keeping those in their own token set means the hero can be tuned without touching anything else.\n\nThe split here mirrors the page: --wf-stage-chrome draws the owl wireframe, the corner ticks and the kicker, so it is GREEN — the owl is the one place this variant should look most like a phosphor screen. The tagline and the caret take --wf-stage-accent, which is cyan, so the two lines of hero copy do not collapse into one colour."
            }
          }
        }
      },
      "stage": {
        "$type": "color",
        "$value": "#030A08",
        "$extensions": {
          "csc": {
            "variants": {
              "hacker-light": {"$value": "#FFFFFF"},
              "cyber": {
                "$value": "rgba( 1, 8, 5, .10 )",
                "comment": ["Translucent, unlike the other two variants: the hero is the largest block on a phone, and an opaque ground made it a lid over the matrix rain. The canvas is fixed behind all content, so opening the hero is what lets the rain read at all on a narrow screen.", "Low on purpose: this is the only thing --wf-stage paints (the home hero ground), and dropping its alpha lets the matrix-rain canvas behind #R-body show through the hero. The fog scrim still darkens the lower band where the copy sits, so text stays legible while the rain reads up top."]
              }
            }
          }
        }
      },
      "stage-fg": {
        "$type": "color",
        "$value": "#E8F4FF",
        "$extensions": {
          "csc": {
            "variants": {"hacker-light": {"$value": "#0A121A"}, "cyber": {"$value": "#9FFFC0"}}
